subsection.p:

          The launcher is the standard startup class for Scope applications. 

          At a minimum, it creates the application's main controller and calls the controller's startup() method. 
          You can use it also to configure Scope with your custom configuration file.

          Below is an example of a launcher class: 

import org.scopemvc.util.*;

public class TCDemoLauncher { 

  public TCDemoLauncher() {} 

  public static void main(String[] args) { 
    // Tell Scope how to load resources (configuration file, images...) from the application jar, 
    // otherwise it will look for the resources into scope-bin.jar and into the default java libraries.
    ResourceLoader.setClientClassLoader(TCDemoLauncher.class.getClassLoader());
    // Use a custom properties file for Scope
    ScopeConfig.setPropertiesName("tc_config");
    // Use a custom resource bundle for the localisation of strings
    UIStrings.setPropertiesName("tc_locale");
    TCDemoController controller = new TCDemoController(); 
    controller.startup(); 
  } 
}


          Starting the application as an Applet or with Java Web Start is not supported directly by Scope. 

          But you can do the following:

public class MyApplet extends JApplet {
  TCDemoController controller;
  public void init() {
    getContentPane().setLayout(new BorderLayout());
    controller = new TCDemoController(); 
    getContentPane().add("Center", (Component)controller.getView());
  }
  public void stop() {
    controller.shutdown();
  }
}
